[{"id":"synth-predict-late","itemId":"synthesisable-subset","role":"warmup","promptHtml":"In simulation, <code class=\"nobreak\">led</code> follows <code class=\"nobreak\">btn</code> 20 ns late, and each step is 10 ns. Steps 0 and 1 are drawn for you. Set <code class=\"nobreak\">led</code> for the rest.","explanationHtml":"<p>20 ns is two steps, so in each step <code class=\"nobreak\">led</code> shows <code class=\"nobreak\">btn</code> from two steps before: <code class=\"nobreak\">btn</code> rises in step 1, so <code class=\"nobreak\">led</code> rises in step 3, and <code class=\"nobreak\">btn</code> falls in step 4, so <code class=\"nobreak\">led</code> falls in step 6. That is the simulator obeying <code class=\"nobreak\">#20</code>. Synthesis builds only a wire from the same line: a pin buffer in and one out, with no delay (lesson 3.4).</p>","type":"waveform","wave":{"id":"late-led","source":"hdl/level-03/late_led/late_led_tb.sv","timescale":"1ps","period":10000,"periodText":"10 ns","unit":"step","cycles":8,"signals":[{"label":"btn","path":"late_led_tb.btn","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[1,"1"],[4,"0"]]},{"label":"led","path":"late_led_tb.led","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[3,"1"],[6,"0"]],"samples":["0","0","0","1","1","1","0","0"]}]},"answer":"led","prefill":[0,1]},{"id":"synth-report-wire","itemId":"synthesisable-subset","role":"core","promptHtml":"Vivado built <code class=\"nobreak\">late_led.sv</code> for the Arty A7-100T. Below is the Slice Logic table of its Utilization report. A slice is one of the small groups of LUTs and flip-flops the chip is built from; the Slice LUTs row counts the LUTs the design uses, and Slice Registers its flip-flops.","explanationHtml":"<p>Read the Used column of the Slice LUTs row, then of the Slice Registers row. Synthesis drops the delay, so <code class=\"nobreak\">led</code> is joined to <code class=\"nobreak\">btn</code> by a wire from one pin buffer to the other, with no logic and no flip-flop between (lesson 3.4). A real delay on purpose would need flip-flops counting clock cycles, and this design has no clock.</p>","type":"report","source":"vivado/reports/late_led/arty-a7-100t/utilization.rpt","firstLine":32,"excerpt":["+-------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","|        Site Type        | Used | Fixed | Prohibited | Available | Util% |","+-------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","| Slice LUTs              |    0 |     0 |          0 |     63400 |  0.00 |","|   LUT as Logic          |    0 |     0 |          0 |     63400 |  0.00 |","|   LUT as Memory         |    0 |     0 |          0 |     19000 |  0.00 |","| Slice Registers         |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","|   Register as Flip Flop |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","|   Register as Latch     |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","| F7 Muxes                |    0 |     0 |          0 |     31700 |  0.00 |","| F8 Muxes                |    0 |     0 |          0 |     15850 |  0.00 |","| Unique Control Sets     |    0 |       |          0 |     15850 |  0.00 |","+-------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+"],"ask":"How many LUTs did Vivado use for the 20 ns delay?","answer":"0","numeric":true},{"id":"synth-find-bug-edges","itemId":"synthesisable-subset","role":"stretch","promptHtml":"This file simulates, and its testbench passes. <code class=\"nobreak\">q_rise</code> should copy <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> at each rising edge and clear at once when <code class=\"nobreak\">rst</code> rises; <code class=\"nobreak\">q_both</code> should copy <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> at both edges of <code class=\"nobreak\">clk</code>. Synthesis refuses to build it. Pick the line that is wrong.","explanationHtml":"<p>The second block asks one flip-flop to act on both edges of the same clock, and a flip-flop is built for one edge, so synthesis refuses it: Yosys stops with &quot;Multiple edge sensitive events&quot;, and Vivado refuses it too (lesson 3.4). The first block also has two events, but the second is a reset on another signal, which the flip-flop's CLR input handles (lesson 2.3), so it builds. Written <code>always_ff @(posedge clk) begin</code>, the second block builds, but copies only once per cycle. To copy twice per cycle you need a clock twice as fast, or special cells at the FPGA's pins that use both edges.</p>","type":"find-bug","file":"hdl/level-03/fast_copy_bug/fast_copy_bug.sv","firstLine":1,"lines":[[{"text":"// Two copies of d. q_rise copies d at each rising edge of clk, and rst","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// clears it at once. q_both copies d at both edges of clk.","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"module","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"fast_copy_bug","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":" (","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" clk,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" rst,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" d,","color":"